      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The base rate/reference class is a gradual spring increase from March to May, but with substantial year-to-year variation: May was 6.697 million in 2023, 6.764 million in 2024, and 6.895 million in 2025. The 2026 January-March level is materially below the corresponding 2025 level, so I do not carry forward the full 2025 May peak."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level and momentum point lower than last year's May value: March 2026 was 6.702 million versus 6.851 million in March 2025, a gap of about 0.149 million. A modest March-to-May rebound of roughly 0.05 million implies approximately 6.75 million for May 2026."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The series is the same national unadjusted Total Participants variant throughout: women, infants, and children participants summed in the official monthly table. No gross-versus-smoothed or seasonally adjusted alternative is used."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence-plus-seasonal model anchored on the March 2026 level and the 2023-2025 May seasonal reference class; the update combines the recent 2026 decline with a modest spring rebound. Successive changes for Oct 2025-Mar 2026 are -0.138204, -0.059123, -0.016311, -0.031720, and +0.037081 million, giving sample sigma = 0.064 million. The 80% half-width is roughly 1.28*sigma = 0.082 million, so 6.750 - 0.082 = 6.668 and 6.750 + 0.082 = 6.832 million."
      },
